attr__` renders with renderfuncs \u2192 `other_renderer(1)` \u2192 `\"<other(1)>\"`. Good.\n\nGreat, that test is correct.\n\nNow, module-level code: the prompt says \"Write as little top-level code as possible, and in particular do not include any top-level code calling into pytest.main or the test itself.\" So: imports, two helper renderer functions, two custom RenderType subclasses, and test functions. That's minimal and necessary. Class definitions at module level are fine (needed for stable identity as dict keys and type checks).\n\nAlternatively, define the rule classes and renderers inside fixtures/tests to minimize top-level code. But classes need identity across the test \u2014 defining them inside each test is fine too since each test is self-contained. Hmm, \"as little top-level code as possible\" \u2014 I could define everything inside test functions. But module-level helper classes/functions are conventional and readable. Let me define the rule classes and renderers at module level (they're not \"code that runs\" per se, just definitions). Actually, to really minimize, I could use a fixture... I think module-level definitions are acceptable and cleaner. But let me reconsider: the instruction emphasizes no top-level code calling pytest.main or the tests. Definitions are fine.\n\nLet me now also double check: `class DummyRule(RenderType): def __init__(self, *args): self.args = list(args)`. `RenderType` has class attr `args: list = []`. Instance attr shadows it. `_render_rules` uses `rule.args` \u2192 instance attr `[1, 2]`. Good.\n\nEdge: `OtherRule(\"x\")` \u2192 args `[\"x\"]`. Good.\n\nNow write the final test file:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests for Register.set_renderfunc in sty/primitive.py (lines 150-156).\"\"\"\n\nfrom sty.primitive import Register\nfrom sty.rendertype import RenderType\n\n\nclass DummyRule(RenderType):\n    \"\"\"A custom render type used by the tests below.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, *args):\n        self.args = list(args)\n\n\nclass OtherRule(RenderType):\n    \"\"\"A second custom render type used by the tests below.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, *args):\n        self.args = list(args)\n\n\ndef dummy_renderer(*args):\n    return \"<dummy({})>\".format(\",\".join(str(a) for a in args))\n\n\ndef other_renderer(*args):\n    return \"<other({})>\".format(\"|\".join(str(a) for a in args))\n\n\ndef test_set_renderfunc_adds_func_and_rerenders_existing_styles():\n    \"\"\"Lines 150-156: adding a renderfunc stores it and re-renders styles.\"\"\"\n    # A locally created register is used so that the global sty registers\n    # (fg, bg, ef, rs) are never touched -> no state pollution.\n    reg = Register()\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {}\n\n    # First call: no Style attributes exist yet, so the loop in\n    # set_renderfunc runs (lines 153-155) but never hits line 156.\n    reg.set_renderfunc(DummyRule, dummy_renderer)\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {DummyRule: dummy_renderer}\n\n    # Attach a Style attribute mixing a registered and an unregistered rule.\n    reg.mystyle = Register.Style(DummyRule(1, 2), OtherRule(\"x\"))\n    assert isinstance(reg.mystyle, Register.Style)\n    assert reg.mystyle == \"<dummy(1,2)>\"\n\n    # Non-Style attributes must never be touched by set_renderfunc.\n    reg.plain = \"keep-me\"\n\n    # Second call: a Style attribute now exists, so line 156 executes and\n    # the style is re-rendered with the newly registered function.\n    reg.set_renderfunc(OtherRule, other_renderer)\n\n    # Line 150: both renderfuncs are stored.\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {DummyRule: dummy_renderer, OtherRule: other_renderer}\n    # Lines 153-156: the style was re-set and re-rendered with both funcs.\n    assert reg.mystyle == \"<dummy(1,2)><other(x)>\"\n    assert isinstance(reg.mystyle, Register.Style)\n    # The styling rules survive the re-render unchanged.\n    assert [type(rule) for rule in reg.mystyle.rules] == [DummyRule, OtherRule]\n    assert reg.mystyle.rules[0].args == [1, 2]\n    assert reg.mystyle.rules[1].args == [\"x\"]\n    # The non-Style attribute is untouched.\n    assert reg.plain == \"keep-me\"\n\n\ndef test_set_renderfunc_replaces_existing_func():\n    \"\"\"Line 150 also covers replacing an already registered renderfunc.\"\"\"\n    reg = Register()\n    reg.set_renderfunc(DummyRule, dummy_renderer)\n    reg.mystyle = Register.Style(DummyRule(7))\n    assert reg.mystyle == \"<dummy(7)>\"\n\n    # Replace the renderfunc for DummyRule; the existing style must be\n    # re-rendered with the new function (lines 153-156).\n    reg.set_renderfunc(DummyRule, other_renderer)\n\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {DummyRule: other_renderer}\n    assert reg.mystyle == \"<other(7)>\"\n    assert isinstance(reg.mystyle, Register.Style)\n    assert reg.mystyle.rules[0].args == [7]\n\n\ndef test_set_renderfunc_rerenders_every_style_attribute():\n    \"\"\"Lines 153-156: all Style attributes are re-rendered, others are not.\"\"\"\n    reg = Register()\n    reg.set_renderfunc(DummyRule, dummy_renderer)\n    reg.style_a = Register.Style(DummyRule(1))\n    reg.style_b = Register.Style(DummyRule(2, 3))\n    reg.plain = \"not-a-style\"\n    assert reg.style_a == \"<dummy(1)>\"\n    assert reg.style_b == \"<dummy(2,3)>\"\n\n    reg.set_renderfunc(DummyRule, other_renderer)\n\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {DummyRule: other_renderer}\n    assert reg.style_a == \"<other(1)>\"\n    assert reg.style_b == \"<other(2,3)>\"\n    assert reg.plain == \"not-a-style\"\n\n\ndef test_set_renderfunc_while_muted_keeps_styles_empty():\n    \"\"\"set_renderfunc must also work while the register is muted.\"\"\"\n    reg = Register()\n    reg.set_renderfunc(DummyRule, dummy_renderer)\n    reg.mystyle = Register.Style(DummyRule(1))\n    assert reg.mystyle == \"<dummy(1)>\"\n\n    reg.mute()\n    assert reg.mystyle == \"\"\n\n    # Re-registering a renderfunc while muted re-sets the styles, which\n    # renders them to empty strings (lines 150-156).\n    reg.set_renderfunc(DummyRule, other_renderer)\n\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {DummyRule: other_renderer}\n    assert reg.mystyle == \"\"\n\n    reg.unmute()\n    assert reg.mystyle == \"<other(1)>\"\n```\n\nWait \u2014 in the muted test, after `reg.mute()`, `reg.mystyle == \"\"`. Then `set_renderfunc` \u2192 `reg.mystyle` re-set while muted \u2192 still \"\". Then `unmute()` \u2192 re-render \u2192 `\"<other(1)>\"`. \n\nHmm, one issue in the muted test: after `mute()`, `reg.mystyle` is `Style(DummyRule(1), value=\"\")`. Then `set_renderfunc`'s loop does `setattr(self, 'mystyle', val)` where val is that style \u2192 `__setattr__` \u2192 muted \u2192 `Style(*value.rules, value=\"\")` \u2192 \"\". Good.\n\nNow, is there any risk that `reg.mute()` doesn't exist or behaves differently? get_info confirmed `mute` and `unmute` exist in the class listing. The real implementation I'm confident about. OK.\n\nOne more consideration \u2014 the `assert reg.renderfuncs == {DummyRule: dummy_renderer}` \u2014 dict equality compares keys by identity/hash (classes are hashable by identity) and values by `==` (functions compare by identity). Good.\n\nAlso, should I worry that `Register.Style` might not be accessible as `Register.Style`? It's a nested class \u2014 yes, `Register.Style` works. get_info for \"Register.Style\" returned \"Unable to obtain information\" but that's just the tool's limitation with nested names; the `Style` get_info returned the nested class source with docstring mentioning `fg.orange = Style(...)`. The `__setattr__` code references `Style` directly (nested scope). So `Register.Style` is correct.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 actually, let me reconsider whether `Style` might be a module-level class in sty/primitive.py rather than nested. The get_info result for `Style` shows:\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Iterable\n\nclass Style(str):\n    \"\"\"...\"\"\"\n    rules: Iterable[StylingRule]\n\n    def __new__(cls, *rules: StylingRule, value: str='') -> 'Style':\n        new_cl