t styling rules for the registers\" \u2014 matches the nested class docstring in sty.\n\nSo `Register.Style` is the right access path. \n\nBut hmm, to be extra safe, could I avoid referencing `Register.Style` explicitly? I could create styles via a helper that gets the Style class from the Register class... no, `Register.Style` is the way. Alternatively `type(reg).Style`? Same thing. Or I could avoid constructing Style directly and instead... no, I need to construct styles. `Register.Style` it is.\n\nActually, one more alternative for robustness: `from sty.primitive import Register` then `Style = Register.Style`. Fine.\n\nNow, let me reconsider the initial assertion `assert reg.renderfuncs == {}` \u2014 `Register()` initializes `renderfuncs = {}`. Good.\n\nAlso \u2014 potential issue: `reg.mystyle = Register.Style(DummyRule(1, 2), OtherRule(\"x\"))` \u2014 `__setattr__` checks `isinstance(value, Style)`. `Register.Style` instance \u2192 True. Then `_render_rules(self.renderfuncs, value.rules)`. At this point renderfuncs = {DummyRule: dummy_renderer}. DummyRule(1,2) \u2192 `dummy_renderer(1, 2)` = `\"<dummy(1,2)>\"`. OtherRule skipped. rendered = `\"<dummy(1,2)>\"`. New Style with value=`\"<dummy(1,2)>\"`. So `reg.mystyle == \"<dummy(1,2)>\"`. \n\nThen `reg.set_renderfunc(OtherRule, other_renderer)`:\n- Line 150: renderfuncs updated.\n- Loop: finds `mystyle` (a Style) \u2192 setattr \u2192 `__setattr__` \u2192 not muted \u2192 `_render_rules` with both funcs \u2192 `\"<dummy(1,2)>\" + \"<other(x)>\"` = `\"<dummy(1,2)><other(x)>\"`. \n\nAssertions check out.\n\nOne more subtle thing: `reg.plain = \"keep-me\"` \u2014 `__setattr__` \u2192 not a Style \u2192 normal set. Then in `set_renderfunc`'s loop, `getattr(reg, 'plain')` = \"keep-me\", not a Style \u2192 skipped. Good.\n\nAlso note: `reg.plain = \"keep-me\"` is set AFTER `reg.mystyle` \u2014 order doesn't matter.\n\nNow, about `dir(self)` and `mystyle`: `dir()` returns sorted attribute names; `mystyle` and `plain` are instance attrs, included. Good.\n\nLet me also think about whether `set_renderfunc` could be affected by `Style` instances being immutable strings \u2014 `setattr` replaces the attribute with a new Style object. Fine.\n\nPotential issue with `assert [type(rule) for rule in reg.mystyle.rules] == [DummyRule, OtherRule]` \u2014 list of classes compared with list of classes \u2014 identity equality. Good.\n\nI think the tests are solid. Let me also add a docstring at top and keep imports minimal. Do I need `import pytest`? Not using any pytest APIs explicitly (no fixtures, no raises). Plain assert-based test functions are collected by pytest. The prompt says \"Create new pytest test functions\" \u2014 plain functions work. But maybe using a fixture for creating a fresh register would be nice and pytest-idiomatic, and also addresses \"clean up after the test\" concerns. Let me add a simple fixture:\n\n```python\n@pytest.fixture()\ndef reg():\n    \"\"\"Provide an isolated Register instance (no global sty state is touched).\"\"\"\n    return Register()\n```\n\nHmm, but then tests take `reg` as a param. That's clean. But it adds `import pytest`. Fine. Actually, is a fixture needed? Each test creates its own register inline. A fixture adds indirection. I'll skip the fixture and create registers inline \u2014 simpler, and each test is self-contained. Actually, let me use the fixture \u2014 it makes the \"isolated instance, no state pollution\" intent explicit and is idiomatic pytest. Hmm. Either way works. I'll go without fixture to keep it dead simple, but add comments about isolation. Actually, you know, let me include the fixture \u2014 the prompt explicitly mentions cleanup/monkeypatch concerns; a fixture returning a fresh Register per test demonstrates good hygiene. But there's nothing to tear down... A fixture without teardown is just a factory. Inline construction is equally clean. I'll go inline, with a comment in each test or one shared comment at top.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider: \"Please also make VERY SURE to clean up after the test, so as to avoid state pollution; use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate.\" \u2014 Since our tests never mutate any global/module state (sty's fg/bg/ef/rs registers are untouched; we only mutate local instances), no cleanup is required. I'll state this in a module docstring comment. That addresses the concern.\n\nFinal check on line numbers: The uncovered lines are 150 (`self.renderfuncs.update(...)`), 153 (`for attr_name in dir(self):`), 154 (`val = getattr(...)`), 155 (`if isinstance(val, Style):`), 156 (`setattr(...)`). Lines 151-152 are the blank/comment lines between. All covered by any `set_renderfunc` call; line 156 requires a Style attribute. \u2713.\n\nAlso both outcomes of the `isinstance` branch at 155: covered (non-Style attrs \u2192 False; mystyle \u2192 True). \u2713\n\nLet me write the final code. Also, should the renderers be defined at module level or inside tests? Module level is fine and keeps tests readable. But \"as little top-level code as possible\" \u2014 definitions are needed; I'll keep them compact. Alternatively define renderers inside tests. I'll define the two rule classes at module level (needed for stable type identity within each test; defining inside each test would also work but duplicates code). Renderers: define inside tests? They're used across tests 1-3... each test could define its own. To minimize top-level code, I could nest everything in tests, but that's verbose. I'll keep module-level helpers \u2014 it's standard practice and the instruction's spirit is \"no top-level execution/pytest.main\", which I satisfy.\n\nActually, let me reconsider using lambdas for renderers to reduce top-level code:\n\n```python\ndummy_renderer = lambda *args: \"<dummy({})>\".format(...)  # noqa: E731\n```\n\nNah, def is cleaner.\n\nOne more thought: should I verify `reg.mystyle.rules` identity preservation more strictly, e.g., `assert reg.mystyle.rules == original_rules` where `original_rules = (DummyRule(1, 2), OtherRule(\"x\"))` captured before? Since tuple equality on custom objects without `__eq__` uses identity, and the same objects are reused, this works. But my type/args assertions are clearer. Keep those.\n\nAlso for test 2: `reg.mystyle.rules[0].args == [7]` \u2014 the rule object is the same `DummyRule(7)` instance created before; its args remain `[7]`. \u2713\n\nDouble-check `other_renderer(7)` \u2192 `\"<other(7)>\"`. \u2713\n\nAnd in test 3: `other_renderer(2, 3)` \u2192 `\"<other(2|3)>\"`. \u2713\n\nFinal code:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"\nTests for `Register.set_renderfunc` in sty/primitive.py.\n\nThe tests only ever mutate *locally created* Register instances, so the\nglobal sty registers (fg, bg, ef, rs) and their renderfuncs are never\ntouched and no cleanup/monkeypatching is required to avoid state pollution.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom sty.primitive import Register\nfrom sty.rendertype import RenderType\n\n\nclass DummyRule(RenderType):\n    \"\"\"Custom render type; instances store their args like the built-in types.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, *args):\n        self.args = list(args)\n\n\nclass OtherRule(RenderType):\n    \"\"\"A second custom render type.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, *args):\n        self.args = list(args)\n\n\ndef dummy_renderer(*args):\n    return \"<dummy({})>\".format(\",\".join(str(a) for a in args))\n\n\ndef other_renderer(*args):\n    return \"<other({})>\".format(\"|\".join(str(a) for a in args))\n\n\ndef test_set_renderfunc_adds_func_and_rerenders_existing_styles():\n    \"\"\"Lines 150-156: a new renderfunc is stored and styles are re-rendered.\"\"\"\n    reg = Register()\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {}\n\n    # First call: no Style attribute exists yet, so the loop over dir(self)\n    # runs (lines 153-155, isinstance-branch False) but line 156 is not hit.\n    reg.set_renderfunc(DummyRule, dummy_renderer)\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {DummyRule: dummy_renderer}\n\n    # Attach a Style attribute that mixes a registered and an unregistered\n    # rule type; only the registered one contributes to the rendered string.\n    reg.mystyle = Register.Style(DummyRule(1, 2), OtherRule(\"x\"))\n    assert isinstance(reg.mystyle, Register.Style)\n    assert reg.mystyle == \"<dummy(1,2)>\"\n\n    # A plain (non-Style) attribute that set_renderfunc must leave alone.\n    reg.plain = \"keep-me\"\n\n    # Second call: a Style attribute now exists, so line 156 executes and\n    # the style is re-rendered with the newly registered function.\n    reg.set_renderfunc(OtherRule, other_renderer)\n\n    # Line 150: the renderfunc mapping contains both entries.\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {\n        DummyRule: dummy_renderer,\n        OtherRule: other_renderer,\n    }\n    # Lines 153-156: the style was re-set and re-rendered with both funcs.\n    assert reg.mystyle == \"<dummy(1,2)><other(x)>\"\n    assert isinstance(reg.mystyle, Register.Style)\n    # The styling rules survive the re-render unchanged.\n    assert [type(rule) for rule in reg.mystyle.rules] == [DummyRule, OtherRule]\n    assert reg.mystyle.rules[0].args == [1, 2]\n    assert reg.mystyle.rules[1].args == [\"x\"]\n    # The non-Style attribute is untouched.\n    assert reg.plain == \"keep-me\"\n\n\ndef test_set_renderfunc_replaces_existing_func():\n    \"\"\"Line 150 also covers replacing an already registered renderfunc.\"\"\"\n    reg = Register()\n    reg.set_renderfunc(DummyRule, dummy_renderer)\n    reg.mystyle = Register.Style(DummyRule(7))\n    assert reg.mystyle == \"<dummy(7)>\"\n\n    # Replace the renderfunc for DummyRule; the existing style must be\n    # re-rendered with the new function (lines 153-156).\n    reg.set_renderfunc(DummyRule, other_renderer)\n\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {DummyRule: other_renderer}\n    assert reg.mystyle == \"<other(7)>\"\n    assert isinstance(reg.mystyle, Register.Style)\n    assert reg.mystyle.rules[0].args == [7]\n\n\ndef test_set_renderfunc_rerenders_every_style_attribute():\n    \"\"\"Lines 153-156: every Style attribute is re-rendered, others are not.\"\"\"\n    reg = Register()\n    reg.set_renderfunc(DummyRule, dummy_renderer)\n    reg.style_a = Register.Style(DummyRule(1))\n    reg.style_b = Register.Style(DummyRule(2, 3))\n    reg.plain = \"not-a-style\"\n    assert reg.style_a == \"<dummy(1)>\"\n    assert reg.style_b == \"<dummy(2,3)>\"\n\n    reg.set_renderfunc(DummyRule, other_renderer)\n\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {DummyRule: other_renderer}\n    assert reg.style_a == \"<other(1)>\"\n    assert reg.style_b == \"<other(2,3)>\"\n    assert reg.plain == \"not-a-style\"\n\n\ndef test_set_renderfunc_while_muted_keeps_styles_empty():\n    \"\"\"set_renderfunc must also re-set (empty) styles while muted.\"\"\"\n    reg = Register()\n    reg.set_renderfunc(DummyRule, dummy_renderer)\n    reg.mystyle = Register.Style(DummyRule(1))\n    assert reg.mystyle == \"<dummy(1)>\"\n\n    reg.mute()\n    assert reg.mystyle == \"\"\n\n    # Re-registering a renderfunc while muted re-sets the styles, which\n    # renders them to empty strings (lines 150-156).\n    reg.set_renderfunc(DummyRule, other_renderer)\n\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {DummyRule: other_renderer}\n    assert reg.mystyle == \"\"\n\n    reg.unmute()\n    assert reg.mystyle == \"<other(1)>\"\n```\n\nWait, one thing in the muted test: after `reg.mute()`, is `reg.mystyle == \"\"` guaranteed? `mute` sets `is_muted = True` then re-sets styles \u2192 `__setattr__` \u2192 muted branch \u2192 `Style(*value.rules, value=\"\")` \u2192 \"\". Yes.\n\nThen `set_renderfunc` while muted: loop \u2192 `setattr(reg, 'mystyle', val)` \u2192 `__setattr__` \u2192 muted \u2192 \"\". \u2713\n\nThen `unmute()`: `is_muted = False`, loop re-sets \u2192 `__setattr__` \u2192 renders with renderfuncs = {DummyRule: other_renderer} \u2192 `other_renderer(1)` = `\"<other(1)>\"`. \u2713\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 in `unmute`, the loop does `setattr(self, attr_name, val)` where val