pty string).\n\nWait, actually, is that right? After mute, `setattr(self, attr_name, val)` where val is the current Style. `__setattr__` with is_muted=True creates `Style(*value.rules, value='')`. So yes, after mute, the style's string value is ''.\n\nHmm wait, but hold on. There's a subtlety: `Style` is a str subclass. `Style(*rules, value='')` creates a str with value ''. So `r.mystyle == ''` \u2192 True. And `isinstance(r.mystyle, Style)` \u2192 True. And `str(r.mystyle)` \u2192 ''. \n\nAlso `r.mystyle.rules` should equal the original rules (same rule objects). I can assert `r.mystyle.rules == original_rules` \u2014 tuple identity/equality. Since the same rule objects are passed through, `r.mystyle.rules == original.rules` compares tuples of the same objects \u2192 True by identity. Actually `==` on tuples compares elements with `==`, which for objects without `__eq__` falls back to identity. Same objects \u2192 True. \n\nBut wait, do I even need the rules assertion? It's a nice postcondition check. Let me include it but carefully: `assert r.mystyle.rules == style.rules` where `style` is the original Style object I created. Since mute passes `*value.rules` (the same rule objects), this holds.\n\nHmm, but one concern: does `Style.rules` exist as an attribute? From the Style source: `setattr(new_cls, 'rules', rules)` in `__new__`. Yes, `rules` attribute exists on Style instances.\n\nNow, the initial set: `r.mystyle = Style(Sgr(31))` while NOT muted. This calls `_render_rules(self.renderfuncs, (Sgr(31),))`. With my renderfunc registered for Sgr, this should produce `\"\\x1b[31m\"` (if called as `renderfunc(*rule.values)` \u2192 `renderfunc(31)` \u2192 `\"\\x1b[31m\"`).\n\nBut what if `_render_rules` calls `renderfunc(rule)` (the rule object)? My defensive renderfunc handles it: `hasattr(rule, 'values')` \u2192 values = (31,) \u2192 `\"\\x1b[31m\"`. Either way, `\"\\x1b[31m\"`. \n\nBut what if `RenderType` doesn't have `.values`? Then `hasattr` fails and I'd render `\"\\x1b[Sgrm\"`... and my assertion `r.mystyle == \"\\x1b[31m\"` would fail. To be safe, maybe I shouldn't assert the exact rendered string before mute. Instead:\n\n- Assert `isinstance(r.mystyle, Style)` before mute.\n- Assert `r.mystyle != ''`? Hmm, if rendering failed silently (rule skipped), it would be ''. But with renderfunc registered, it should render. Ugh.\n\nOK let me think about what I'm most confident about. Actually, let me reconsider: I'm quite confident about the sty internals now. Let me reconstruct the full sty/primitive.py from memory (sty 1.0.4, the latest release):\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\nfrom typing import Callable, Deque, Dict, Iterable, Tuple, Type, Union\n\nfrom .rendertype import RenderType, StylingRule\n\nRenderfuncs = Dict[Type[RenderType], Callable[..., str]]\n\n\ndef _render_rules(\n    renderfuncs: Renderfuncs, rules: Iterable[StylingRule]\n) -> Tuple[str, Iterable[StylingRule]]:\n    rendered: Deque[str] = deque()\n    rendered_rules: Deque[StylingRule] = deque()\n    for rule in rules:\n        if isinstance(rule, StylingRule):\n            if type(rule) in renderfuncs:\n                rendered.append(renderfuncs[type(rule)](*rule.values))\n                rendered_rules.append(rule)\n        else:\n            rendered.append(rule)\n            rendered_rules.append(rule)\n    return \"\".join(rendered), rendered_rules\n\n\nclass Style(str):\n    \"\"\"\n    This type stores the different styling rules for the registers and the resulting\n    ANSI-sequence as a string.\n    ...\n    \"\"\"\n\n    rules: Iterable[StylingRule]\n\n    def __new__(cls, *rules: StylingRule, value: str = \"\") -> \"Style\":\n        new_cls = str.__new__(cls, value)\n        setattr(new_cls, \"rules\", rules)\n        return new_cls\n\n\nclass Register:\n    \"\"\"\n    This is the base Register class. All default registers (fg, bg, ef, rs) are\n    created from this class. You can use it to create your own custom registers.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.renderfuncs: Renderfuncs = {}\n        self.is_muted = False\n        self.eightbit_call = lambda x: x\n        self.rgb_call = lambda r, g, b: (r, g, b)\n\n    def __setattr__(self, name: str, value: Style):\n        if isinstance(value, Style):\n            if self.is_muted:\n                rendered_style = Style(*value.rules, value=\"\")\n            else:\n                rendered, rules = _render_rules(self.renderfuncs, value.rules)\n                rendered_style = Style(*rules, value=rendered)\n            return super().__setattr__(name, rendered_style)\n        else:\n            return super().__setattr__(name, value)\n\n    def __call__(self, *args: Union[int, str], **kwargs) -> str:\n        if self.is_muted:\n            return \"\"\n        len_args = len(args)\n        if len_args == 1:\n            if isinstance(args[0], int):\n                return self.eightbit_call(*args, **kwargs)\n            else:\n                return getattr(self, args[0])\n        elif len_args == 3:\n            return self.rgb_call(*args, **kwargs)\n        else:\n            return \"\"\n\n    def set_eightbit_call(self, rendertype: Type[RenderType]) -> None:\n        ...\n\n    def set_rgb_call(self, rendertype: Type[RenderType]) -> None:\n        ...\n\n    def set_renderfunc(self, rendertype: Type[RenderType], func: Callable) -> None:\n        self.renderfuncs.update({rendertype: func})\n        for attr_name in dir(self):\n            val = getattr(self, attr_name)\n            if isinstance(val, Style):\n                setattr(self, attr_name, val)\n\n    def mute(self) -> None:\n        self.is_muted = True\n        for attr_name in dir(self):\n            val = getattr(self, attr_name)\n            if isinstance(val, Style):\n                setattr(self, attr_name, val)\n\n    def unmute(self) -> None:\n        self.is_muted = False\n        for attr_name in dir(self):\n            val = getattr(self, attr_name)\n            if isinstance(val, Style):\n                setattr(self, attr_name, val)\n\n    def as_dict(self) -> Dict[str, str]:\n        ...\n\n    def as_namedtuple(self):\n        ...\n\n    def copy(self) -> \"Register\":\n        ...\n```\n\nThis matches the tool outputs exactly. Great. And `renderfuncs[type(rule)](*rule.values)` \u2014 I'm fairly confident.\n\nAnd sty/rendertype.py:\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Iterable, Tuple, Union\n\n\nclass RenderType:\n    def __init__(self, *values: Union[int, str]):\n        self.values = values\n\n\nclass StylingRule(RenderType):\n    def __init__(self, *values: Union[int, str]):\n        super().__init__(*values)\n\n\nclass Sgr(StylingRule):\n    def __init__(self, *values: int):\n        super().__init__(*values)\n\n\nclass EightbitFg(StylingRule):\n    def __init__(self, num: int):\n        super().__init__(num)\n\n\nclass EightbitBg(StylingRule):\n    def __init__(self, num: int):\n        super().__init__(num)\n\n\nclass RgbFg(StylingRule):\n    def __init__(self, r: int, g: int, b: int):\n        super().__init__(r, g, b)\n\n\nclass RgbBg(StylingRule):\n    def __init__(self, r: int, g: int, b: int):\n        super().__init__(r, g, b)\n\n\nclass RgbColor(StylingRule):\n    ...\n```\n\nSomething like that. OK.\n\nNow, the test design. I want to cover lines 163-168 of `mute`:\n\n```python\n163:         self.is_muted = True\n165:         for attr_name in dir(self):\n166:             val = getattr(self, attr_name)\n167:             if isinstance(val, Style):\n168:                 setattr(self, attr_name, val)\n```\n\nTest plan:\n\n```python\nimport sty\nfrom sty import Register, Style\nfrom sty.rendertype import Sgr\n\n\ndef _sgr_renderfunc(*args):\n    \"\"\"Render an Sgr rule to an ANSI escape sequence.\n\n    Accepts either the rule object itself or its unpacked values, depending\n    on how sty's internal _render_rules invokes registered renderfuncs.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(args) == 1 and hasattr(args[0], \"values\"):\n        values = tuple(args[0].values)\n    else:\n        values = args\n    return \"\\x1b[\" + \";\".join(str(v) for v in values) + \"m\"\n\n\ndef test_mute_covers_all_lines(tmp_path=None):\n    r = Register()\n    r.set_renderfunc(Sgr, _sgr_renderfunc)\n    r.red = Style(Sgr(31))\n    r.bold = Style(Sgr(1))\n    \n    # Pre-conditions\n    assert r.is_muted is False\n    assert isinstance(r.red, Style)\n    assert r.red == \"\\x1b[31m\"\n    assert r.bold == \"\\x1b[1m\"\n    \n    r.mute()\n    \n    # Post-conditions\n    assert r.is_muted is True\n    assert isinstance(r.red, Style)\n    assert r.red == \"\"  # muted styles render as empty strings\n    assert isinstance(r.bold, Style)\n    assert r.bold == \"\"\n    # The styling rules are preserved on the muted style objects.\n    assert r.red.rules == (Sgr(31),)  # hmm, equality issue\n```\n\nWait, `r.red.rules == (Sgr(31),)` \u2014 the rules stored are the SAME Sgr(31) object I created (passed through `*value.rules`). So comparing with a NEW `Sgr(31)` would use `__eq__` which defaults to identity \u2192 False! I must keep a reference to the original rule:\n\n```python\nred_rule = Sgr(31)\nr.red = Style(red_rule)\n...\nassert r.red.rules == (red_rule,)\n```\n\nSince it's the same object, tuple equality via identity works. Actually, even simpler: `assert r.red.rules == original_style.rules` where original_style is the Style object I created. The rules tuple contains the same objects.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's a subtlety with `r.red = Style(Sgr(31))`: `__setattr__` re-renders and creates a NEW Style: `Style(*rules, value=rendered)` where rules comes from `_render_rules`. The rendered_rules deque contains the same rule objects. So `r.red.rules` is a deque converted to... hmm, `Style(*rules, ...)` \u2014 `rules` is a deque, and `*rules` unpacks it, so `Style.__new__` receives `*rules` as varargs \u2192 `rules` attribute is a tuple of the same rule objects. Good.\n\nSo after mute: `r.red.rules` should be a tuple containing the same `Sgr(31)` object. So:\n\n```python\nrule = Sgr(31)\nr.red = Style(rule)\nassert r.red.rules == (rule,)  # True by identity\nr.mute()\nassert r.red.rules == (rule,)  # Still True \u2014 rules preserved\n```\n\nNow, about `dir(self)` and the loop: `dir(r)` includes 'bold', 'red', 'renderfuncs', 'is_muted', 'eightbit_call', 'rgb_call', plus all methods and dunders. `getattr` on all of these works. Only 'red' and 'bold' are Styles \u2192 line 168 executes twice. \n\nAlso, I should test `unmute` too? The task says cover lines 163-168 (mute). But testing unmute as well is good for completeness and it's the natural counterpart. The task says \"Create new pytest test functions that execute all missing lines and branches\". The missing lines are only in `mute`. But adding an unmute test doesn't hurt. Actually, let me focus on mute but also verify the muted register behaves correctly (e.g., `r('red')` returns '' when muted, per `__call__`).\n\nHmm wait, `r('red')` when muted returns ''. When not muted, `r('red')` returns `getattr(r, 'red')` = the ANSI sequence. That's a nice behavioral postcondition:\n\n```python\nassert r(\"red\") == \"\\x1b[31m\"  # before mute\nr.mute()\nassert r(\"red\") == \"\"  # after mute\n```\n\nThat's a great end-to-end postcondition demonstrating muting works.\n\nNow, state pollution: I'm creating a local `Register()` instance, not touching globals. No cleanup needed. But to be extra careful, I don't import and mutate `sty.fg` etc. Good.\n\nShould I also test `unmute` to restore? Since the register is local, no need. But testing unmute adds coverage for unmute's lines (which are presumably already covered by existing tests? The task says only 163-168 are missing, which is mute. Unmute must be covered already, or maybe not \u2014 the task only mentions 163-168). I'll include an unmute check as part of the same test to verify round-trip behavior, which also strengthens correctness. Actually wait \u2014 careful: if u