necessary.\n\nOne more consideration: the loop in mute iterates `dir(self)`. To make sure line 167's False branch is also exercised (it is, for non-Style attrs) and True branch (for Style attrs). Both covered by having a mix. Good.\n\nAlso \u2014 potential gotcha: `getattr(self, attr_name)` for attr_name like `__class__`, `__delattr__`, etc. \u2014 all fine. What about `__setattr__` itself? `getattr(r, '__setattr__')` returns a bound method \u2014 fine, not a Style.\n\nWait, one more: does `dir(self)` include `__dict__`? For a normal instance, yes: `dir(obj)` includes '__dict__' and '__weakref__' if the class doesn't define `__slots__`. `getattr(r, '__dict__')` \u2192 the instance dict \u2192 not a Style \u2192 fine.\n\nNow let me also think: are there any OTHER missing lines/branches related to mute? The task says lines 163-168. The `if isinstance(val, Style):` branch \u2014 both True and False paths get exercised in my test (False for non-Style attrs like renderfuncs, True for red/bold). \n\nLet me also double-check the mute flow with my test register:\n\n1. `r = Register()` \u2192 `__init__` sets renderfuncs={}, is_muted=False, eightbit_call, rgb_call. Note: `__setattr__` is called for each; none are Style instances \u2192 `super().__setattr__`. But wait \u2014 `__setattr__` checks `isinstance(value, Style)` first; for `{}`, False, `False`, lambdas \u2192 else branch \u2192 `super().__setattr__(name, value)`. Fine. But hold on: `self.is_muted` is accessed in `__setattr__`? No! Looking at the actual `__setattr__` from the tool:\n\n```python\ndef __setattr__(self, name: str, value: Style):\n    if isinstance(value, Style):\n        if self.is_muted:\n            ...\n```\n\n`self.is_muted` is only accessed when value is a Style. During `__init__`, no Style values are set, so no AttributeError. \n\n2. `r.set_renderfunc(Sgr, func)` \u2192 updates renderfuncs, loops dir(self) \u2014 no Style attrs yet \u2192 nothing.\n\n3. `r.red = Style(rule)` \u2192 `__setattr__`: isinstance Style \u2192 True; `self.is_muted` \u2192 False \u2192 `_render_rules(self.renderfuncs, value.rules)` \u2192 renders `\"\\x1b[31m\"` \u2192 `super().__setattr__('red', Style(rule, value=\"\\x1b[31m\"))`. \n\nWait, `Style(*rules, value=rendered)` \u2014 `rules` here is the returned `rendered_rules` (a deque?). `Style.__new__(cls, *rules, value='')` \u2192 `setattr(new_cls, 'rules', rules)` where rules is the varargs tuple. So `r.red.rules` is a tuple containing `rule`. Good.\n\n4. Assertions before mute: `r.red == \"\\x1b[31m\"`, `isinstance(r.red, Style)`, `r.is_muted is False`, `r(\"red\") == \"\\x1b[31m\"`.\n\nHmm, `r(\"red\")` \u2192 `__call__` with args=('red',) \u2192 not muted \u2192 len_args == 1, not int \u2192 `getattr(self, 'red')` \u2192 `\"\\x1b[31m\"`. \u2713\n\n5. `r.mute()`:\n   - Line 163: `self.is_muted = True` \u2192 `__setattr__('is_muted', True)` \u2192 not a Style \u2192 `super().__setattr__`. \u2713\n   - Lines 165-168: loop over dir(self). For 'red': val is Style \u2192 line 168: `setattr(r, 'red', val)` \u2192 `__setattr__`: isinstance Style \u2192 `self.is_muted` \u2192 True \u2192 `rendered_style = Style(*value.rules, value='')` \u2192 `super().__setattr__('red', Style(rule, value=''))`. \u2713 Line 168 covered!\n   - Same for 'bold' if I add it.\n\n6. Post-mute assertions:\n   - `r.is_muted is True` \u2713\n   - `r.red == \"\"` \u2713 (Style with empty value)\n   - `isinstance(r.red, Style)` \u2713\n   - `r.red.rules == (rule,)` \u2713 (rules preserved)\n   - `r(\"red\") == \"\"` \u2713 (muted register call returns '')\n   - `str(r.red) == \"\"` \u2014 redundant with ==.\n\n7. Optionally `r.unmute()`:\n   - `is_muted = False`, loop: setattr('red', val) \u2192 not muted \u2192 `_render_rules` \u2192 re-renders `\"\\x1b[31m\"`.\n   - Assert `r.red == \"\\x1b[31m\"` and `r(\"red\") == \"\\x1b[31m\"` and `r.is_muted is False`.\n\nThis gives a nice round-trip test. \n\nNow, about test structure: the task says \"Create new pytest test functions\" (plural). Let me write maybe two tests:\n\n1. `test_mute_covers_missing_lines` \u2014 the main test covering mute with Style attributes (lines 163-168, both branches of the isinstance check).\n2. `test_mute_then_unmute_roundtrip` \u2014 mute then unmute, verifying restoration.\n\nOr combine. Hmm, \"always making sure that each test is correct and indeed improves coverage\". Both tests would use a local Register \u2014 no state pollution. \n\nWait, should I worry that `Register()` instantiation itself might have uncovered lines? Not my concern \u2014 only 163-168.\n\nLet me also consider: should the tests avoid importing `sty` package `__init__` side effects? `import sty` runs `sty/__init__.py` which creates fg, bg, ef, rs registers. That's fine \u2014 read-only for us.\n\nHmm, one thing: `from sty import Register, Style` \u2014 both are in sty/__init__ (exported). Yes, the README shows `from sty import fg, bg, ef, rs, Style, RgbFg, Register`. Actually let me double check: sty's `__init__.py` does `from .primitive import Register, Style` \u2014 I believe so. The get_info for Register showed the class defined with `from typing import ...` imports, suggesting it's in primitive.py. And `sty.Register` should be accessible. To be safe: `import sty` then use `sty.Register`, `sty.Style`. Or `from sty import Register, Style`. Both should work. I'll use `from sty import Register, Style` and `from sty.rendertype import Sgr`.\n\nHmm, actually, wait. Let me reconsider whether `Sgr` is exported from `sty` directly. In sty/__init__.py (v1.0.4):\n\n```python\nfrom .rendertype import *\nfrom .primitive import Register, Style\n```\n\nHmm, and rendertype.py might not define `__all__`, so `*` imports all public names. `Sgr` is public. So `from sty import Sgr` works. But `from sty.rendertype import Sgr` is guaranteed. I'll use the latter for safety. Actually, hmm, both are fine; I'll use `from sty.rendertype import Sgr` since the tool output confirmed `sty.rendertype` exists with RenderType.\n\nNow the renderfunc. Let me write it defensively but simply:\n\n```python\ndef _render_sgr(*args):\n    \"\"\"Render an Sgr rule into an ANSI escape sequence.\n\n    sty may invoke registered renderfuncs either with the rule object or with\n    its unpacked values; support both to keep the test robust.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(args) == 1 and hasattr(args[0], \"values\"):\n        values = tuple(args[0].values)\n    else:\n        values = args\n    return \"\\x1b[\" + \";\".join(str(v) for v in values) + \"m\"\n```\n\nAnd assertion: `r.red == \"\\x1b[31m\"`.\n\nHmm, wait. What if `_render_rules` skips rules whose type is not in renderfuncs... I registered Sgr, so fine. What if `_render_rules` raises KeyError for unregistered? Not relevant since registered.\n\nWhat if `_render_rules` calls `renderfunc(rule.values)` (passing the tuple as single arg)? \u2192 args = ((31,),) \u2192 len==1, hasattr(tuple, 'values') \u2192 False \u2192 values = ((31,),) \u2192 `\"\\x1b[31m\"`? `str((31,))` = \"(31,)\" \u2192 `\"\\x1b[(31,)m\"`. \u2717. Hmm. But that calling convention is unlikely. The README lambda `lambda num: ...` implies unpacked values. I'll go with unpacked + rule-object handling.\n\nActually, hmm, let me simplify: maybe I should make the assertion on the pre-mute rendered value less strict, e.g., just assert it's a non-empty Style:\n\n```python\nassert isinstance(r.red, Style)\nassert r.red != \"\"\n```\n\nIf rendering works (which it should), r.red is non-empty. If my renderfunc convention is wrong, r.red might be `\"\\x1b[(31,)m\"` \u2014 still non-empty, test still passes, and mute coverage still achieved. The critical postconditions are about mute behavior, which don't depend on the exact rendered string:\n\n- After mute: `r.red == \"\"` \u2014 this holds regardless of the pre-mute rendered value, because mute's `__setattr__` path (is_muted=True) creates `Style(*value.rules, value='')` without rendering. \u2713 Robust!\n- `r.red.rules == (rule