packed constructor values; handle both calling conventions.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(args) == 1 and hasattr(args[0], \"values\"):\n        values = tuple(args[0].values)\n    else:\n        values = args\n    return \"\\x1b[\" + \";\".join(str(v) for v in values) + \"m\"\n\n\ndef _make_register():\n    \"\"\"Create a fresh Register with a couple of Style attributes.\"\"\"\n    reg = Register()\n    reg.set_renderfunc(Sgr, _render_sgr)\n    red_rule = Sgr(31)\n    bold_rule = Sgr(1)\n    reg.red = Style(red_rule)\n    reg.bold = Style(bold_rule)\n    return reg, red_rule, bold_rule\n\n\ndef test_mute_sets_flag_and_blanks_style_attributes():\n    reg, red_rule, bold_rule = _make_register()\n\n    # Pre-conditions: the register is unmuted and the styles render.\n    assert reg.is_muted is False\n    assert isinstance(reg.red, Style)\n    assert isinstance(reg.bold, Style)\n    assert reg.red != \"\"\n    assert reg.bold != \"\"\n\n    reg.mute()\n\n    # Post-conditions: mute() flipped the flag (line 163) ...\n    assert reg.is_muted is True\n\n    # ... walked all attributes (lines 165-166) and re-set the Style ones\n    # (lines 167-168). Muted styles keep their rules but render to ''.\n    assert isinstance(reg.red, Style)\n    assert reg.red == \"\"\n    assert reg.red.rules == (red_rule,)\n\n    assert isinstance(reg.bold, Style)\n    assert reg.bold == \"\"\n    assert reg.bold.rules == (bold_rule,)\n\n    # A muted register returns an empty string when called.\n    assert reg(\"red\") == \"\"\n\n\ndef test_mute_then_unmute_restores_rendering():\n    reg, _red_rule, _bold_rule = _make_register()\n    reg.mute()\n    assert reg.is_muted is True\n    assert reg.red == \"\"\n\n    reg.unmute()\n\n    assert reg.is_muted is False\n    # The styles are re-rendered from their preserved rules.\n    assert isinstance(reg.red, Style)\n    assert reg.red != \"\"\n    assert reg(\"red\") == reg.red\n```\n\nWait \u2014 in `test_mute_then_unmute_restores_rendering`, after unmute, `reg.red` is re-rendered via `_render_rules`. If my renderfunc convention assumption is wrong, `reg.red` might be some other non-empty string, and `reg(\"red\") == reg.red` still holds (since `__call__` returns `getattr(self, 'red')`). And `reg.red != \"\"` holds as long as rendering produces something non-empty. If `_render_rules` skipped the rule (type not in renderfuncs \u2014 but it IS registered), it'd be ''. Registered \u2192 rendered. OK.\n\nHmm, but wait: is `reg(\"red\")` valid? `__call__` with args=('red',): `if self.is_muted: return ''` \u2014 not muted \u2192 `len_args == 1` \u2192 `isinstance('red', int)` False \u2192 `getattr(self, 'red')`. \u2713\n\nAlso in the muted test: `reg(\"red\")` \u2192 is_muted \u2192 `''`. \u2713\n\nNow \u2014 state pollution check: \n- I create local Register instances. No globals mutated. \n- No monkeypatch needed since nothing global is touched. \n- `import sty` executes module init (creates fg/bg/ef/rs) \u2014 read-only usage.\n\nThe task says \"Be sure to clean up after the test... use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate.\" Since I don't touch global state, no cleanup needed. But maybe I should demonstrate good practice by using monkeypatch for safety? Hmm \u2014 there's nothing to patch. Local registers are inherently isolated. I could add a comment explaining that. Actually, one could argue: to be extra safe against accidental global mutation, I could snapshot... no, unnecessary. The tests only use local objects.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, let me reconsider one thing: should I use `sty.fg` instead to be more \"realistic\"? No \u2014 local Register is cleaner and pollution-free.\n\nOne more check on `reg.red.rules == (red_rule,)`: \n\n- `reg.red = Style(red_rule)` \u2192 `__setattr__` \u2192 not muted \u2192 `_render_rules(reg.renderfuncs, (red_rule,))` \u2192 returns `(rendered, rendered_rules)` where rendered_rules contains red_rule \u2192 `Style(*rendered_rules, value=rendered)` \u2192 `reg.red.rules == (