hing. But an extra simple test doesn't hurt. Hmm, the instruction says \"Create new pytest test functions that execute all missing lines and branches\" \u2014 plural functions. I'll include 2-3 tests.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider `reg(1)`: `__call__` \u2192 `if self.is_muted: return ''`. Yes \u2192 ''. \u2713\n\nLet me also double check `reg(\"red\")` before mute in test 1... I have `assert reg(\"red\") == reg.red`? I put that in test 2 after unmute. In test 1 pre-conditions, I could add `assert reg(\"red\") == reg.red` \u2014 wait, `reg(\"red\")` returns `getattr(reg, 'red')` which IS reg.red. Trivially true. It does verify `__call__` dispatch works. Fine to include or not. I'll include `assert reg(\"red\") != \"\"` pre-mute... eh. Let me keep it simple: pre-mute assert `reg(\"red\") == reg.red` (documents the unmuted dispatch), post-mute `reg(\"red\") == \"\"`.\n\nHmm, one concern about `reg(\"red\") == reg.red`: `getattr(self, args[0])` returns the Style; comparing Style == Style \u2192 str equality \u2192 both are the same object \u2192 True. \u2713\n\nNow, let me also think about whether `set_renderfunc` before setting styles could cause issues: `set_renderfunc` loops dir(self) and setattrs Style attrs \u2014 none exist yet. \u2713\n\nWhat about the order: `reg.red = Style(red_rule)` \u2014 at this point renderfuncs has Sgr \u2192 renders. \u2713\n\nAlright. One more paranoid check on `_render_rules` return: `rendered, rules = _render_rules(...)`. If `_render_rules` returns rules as a deque, `Style(*rules, value=rendered)` unpacks fine. \u2713\n\nAnd `hasattr(args[0], \"values\")` \u2014 for `Sgr` instance, `.values` = (31,). If RenderType doesn't define `.values`... then `hasattr` False \u2192 values = (rule,) \u2192 `str(rule)`. What would `str(Sgr(31))` be? Default object repr: `<sty.rendertype.Sgr object at 0x...>`. Then `reg.red` = `\"\\x1b[<sty.rendertype.Sgr object at 0x...>m\"` \u2014 non-empty, so `reg.red != \"\"` passes; mute still blanks it;